;; FH-STRUCT-011 — one `v/custom-element` declaration, every lowering path.
;;
;; A web component's rich inputs are JS PROPERTIES: `el.scale = {…}` is how a
;; map gets in, and nothing about the keyword `:scale` says so. Undeclared, the
;; name travels the attribute grammar, whose rule for an unrecognized kebab
;; spelling is to pass it through VERBATIM — so React writes the attribute, the
;; element's property setter never runs, and the component mounts renderable but
;; INERT with nothing reported. That silence is why the declaration exists.
;;
;; WHY THE CASE TABLE IS SHAPED BY PATH RATHER THAN BY OUTCOME. Every row below
;; pins the SAME classification, and the rows differ only in how the prop
;; reached the element: a compiled literal props map (classified at build time
;; under that build's own declaration slice), the interpreted walk, a
;; `v/spread` beside a compiled element, a `v/spread` in an interpreted body, a
;; `v/spread-safe` caller. That redundancy is the whole subject. The
;; machinery's first consumer was the compiled literal map alone, and a rule
;; reaching only that one means *property* at a literal site and *attribute*
;; one `v/spread` away — one declaration, two meanings, no diagnostic. So the
;; fixture is written to go RED at whichever path stops asking.
;;
;; THE ADVERSARIAL ROWS.
;;
;;   `:scale` carries a MAP. It is legal only because it is declared: the
;;   `:refused` rows below show the identical value on an undeclared name
;;   failing loud as an attribute, on a declared element and an undeclared one
;;   alike. A property's meaning belongs to the element's own setter, which is
;;   why the attribute-value grammar has no opinion about it.
;;
;;   `:tab-index` on `:ce-attrish` is a declared name that is ALSO a standard
;;   HTML attribute spelling. The declaration is the SOLE classifier, so it is
;;   the `tabIndex` property here and never the `tabindex` attribute — a
;;   classification that consulted an attribute-name table would get this
;;   backwards and be right about everything else.
;;
;;   `:ce-below` is declared BELOW the views that use it. Macros expand top to
;;   bottom, so before the source harvest a view above its declaration baked
;;   the ATTRIBUTE lowering and the same two forms reversed baked the property
;;   one. Declaration order is not an authoring decision.
;;
;;   `:ce-plain` is declared nowhere. An undeclared ELEMENT needs no
;;   declaration at all, so declaring is only ever the exception.
;;
;; THE MARKUP ROWS ARE THE SERVER'S HALF, and they are pinned as whole HTML
;; strings because the claim is about what is NOT in them. A server cannot set
;; a property, so a property-classified name is omitted from markup under EVERY
;; spelling and applied by the client at hydration; a present-and-wrong
;; attribute would hydrate without complaint and leave the component inert.
;;
;; THE DOM ROWS close the loop on a real element in a real browser, which is
;; the only place "it was set as a property, not an attribute" is observable.
{:fh/id "FH-STRUCT-011"
 :fh/law
 "A `(v/custom-element tag {:properties #{…}})` declaration classifies a prop identically on every lowering path — a compiled literal props map, a compiled element's dynamic props, the interpreted walk, `v/spread` and `v/spread-safe` — because the classification belongs to the declaration rather than to the tier that saw the prop. A declared name keeps its authored kebab key in the structural tree, keeps its value verbatim outside the attribute-value grammar, is named in the node's reserved `:rf.ui/property-props` set, is omitted from server markup under every spelling, and is applied in the browser as the ruled kebab -> camelCase JS property. Every other name is an attribute, an undeclared element is all-attributes, and declaration ORDER is not an authoring decision."

 ;; The declarations the case table is written against, all from the one
 ;; source `re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views`. Asserted against the live
 ;; registry, so a fixture written for a declaration the source no longer makes
 ;; cannot pass by reading its own expectations back.
 :declarations
 [{:tag :ce-panel   :properties #{:accent-color :help-text :scale}}
  {:tag :ce-attrish :properties #{:tab-index}}
  {:tag :ce-below   :properties #{:model}}]

 ;; The tag that is deliberately absent from the registry.
 :undeclared-tag :ce-plain

 :cases
 [{:note "the interpreted walk: three declared names are properties, the map value among them; the undeclared names are attributes"
   :view :panel-literal
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/panel-literal
          :children [{:tag :ce-panel
                      :attrs {:accent-color "blue" :help-text "h"
                              :scale {:min 0 :max 10}
                              :data-x "d" :label "lab"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:accent-color :help-text :scale}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "the same body promoted with `{:compiled true}` — the build-time classification answers the same tree"
   :view :panel-literal-compiled
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/panel-literal-compiled
          :children [{:tag :ce-panel
                      :attrs {:accent-color "blue" :help-text "h"
                              :scale {:min 0 :max 10}
                              :data-x "d" :label "lab"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:accent-color :help-text :scale}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "`v/spread` in an interpreted body: a map the compiler never saw, classified by the declaration's runtime arm"
   :view :panel-spread
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/panel-spread
          :children [{:tag :ce-panel
                      :attrs {:accent-color "blue" :help-text "h"
                              :scale {:min 0 :max 10}
                              :data-x "d" :label "lab"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:accent-color :help-text :scale}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "`v/spread` beside a COMPILED element — the dynamic props of a compiled site reach the same runtime arm"
   :view :panel-spread-compiled
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/panel-spread-compiled
          :children [{:tag :ce-panel
                      :attrs {:accent-color "blue" :help-text "h"
                              :scale {:min 0 :max 10}
                              :data-x "d" :label "lab"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:accent-color :help-text :scale}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "`v/spread-safe`: a forwarded DECLARED name is still a property, the owned prop still wins, and the forwarded undeclared name is still an attribute"
   :view :panel-spread-safe
   :args [{:caller {:accent-color "caller-blue" :label "ignored" :data-y "y"}}]
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/panel-spread-safe
          :props {:caller {:accent-color "caller-blue" :label "ignored" :data-y "y"}}
          :children [{:tag :ce-panel
                      :attrs {:accent-color "caller-blue" :label "owned" :data-y "y"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:accent-color}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "nil-is-absent runs FIRST: a conditional property the element does not carry is not a property of this element"
   :view :panel-nil-property
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/panel-nil-property
          :children [{:tag :ce-panel
                      :attrs {:help-text "h" :data-x "d"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:help-text}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "the declaration is the SOLE classifier: a declared standard-attribute spelling is a property, and the undeclared name beside it stays an attribute"
   :view :attrish
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/attrish
          :children [{:tag :ce-attrish
                      :attrs {:tab-index "3" :role "note"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:tab-index}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "an UNDECLARED element is all-attributes: no declaration, so no property marker at all — absent, never an empty set"
   :view :undeclared
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/undeclared
          :children [{:tag :ce-plain :attrs {:accent-color "x" :data-y "y"}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "declaration order: the view sits ABOVE its declaration and classifies identically (interpreted)"
   :view :below-literal
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/below-literal
          :children [{:tag :ce-below
                      :attrs {:model "m" :data-x "d"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:model}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "declaration order: and compiled, where the classification is baked at macroexpansion and the harvest is what makes it right"
   :view :below-literal-compiled
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/below-literal-compiled
          :children [{:tag :ce-below
                      :attrs {:model "m" :data-x "d"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:model}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}

  {:note "declaration order: and through `v/spread`, whose runtime arm cannot be order-dependent and must agree anyway"
   :view :below-spread
   :tree {:view-id :re-frame.freehand.custom-element-views/below-spread
          :children [{:tag :ce-below
                      :attrs {:model "m" :data-x "d"}
                      :rf.ui/property-props #{:model}}]
          :rf.ui/tree-version 1}}]

 ;; The ruled kebab -> camelCase JS property name — the spelling the client
 ;; SETS. Deliberately not react-dom's attribute table: that table's rule for
 ;; an unrecognized kebab name is to pass it through, which is the whole defect
 ;; the declaration exists to prevent.
 :property-names
 [{:note "the everyday case"                                  :attr :accent-color :property "accentColor"}
  {:note "and again, so one row is not the whole claim"        :attr :help-text    :property "helpText"}
  {:note "a single-word name is already its own property name" :attr :scale        :property "scale"}
  {:note "a standard-attribute spelling still camelizes"       :attr :tab-index    :property "tabIndex"}]

 ;; A value outside the attribute-value grammar is legal as a declared PROPERTY
 ;; and refused as an attribute — on a declared element and an undeclared one
 ;; alike, because what makes it legal is the NAME being declared, never the
 ;; element merely having a declaration.
 :refused
 [{:note "the same map value on an undeclared name of a DECLARED element"
   :form [:ce-panel {:nope {:min 0}}]
   :id   :rf.error/ui-tree-malformed}
  {:note "and on an undeclared element, where nothing is a property"
   :form [:ce-plain {:scale {:min 0}}]
   :id   :rf.error/ui-tree-malformed}]

 ;; The SERVER's half, pinned whole because the claim is about absence. A
 ;; property-classified name appears under NO spelling; the attributes beside
 ;; it are unaffected.
 :markup
 [{:note "interpreted: the three properties omitted, the two attributes emitted"
   :view :panel-literal          :html "<ce-panel data-x=\"d\" label=\"lab\"></ce-panel>"}
  {:note "compiled: byte-identical markup — the modes cannot disagree about the server"
   :view :panel-literal-compiled :html "<ce-panel data-x=\"d\" label=\"lab\"></ce-panel>"}
  {:note "`v/spread`: byte-identical again, which is the literal-vs-spread parity claim at the serialiser"
   :view :panel-spread           :html "<ce-panel data-x=\"d\" label=\"lab\"></ce-panel>"}
  {:note "`v/spread` beside a compiled element: the fourth path, same markup"
   :view :panel-spread-compiled  :html "<ce-panel data-x=\"d\" label=\"lab\"></ce-panel>"}
  {:note "a declared standard-attribute spelling is omitted — NOT emitted as `tabindex`"
   :view :attrish                :html "<ce-attrish role=\"note\"></ce-attrish>"}
  {:note "an undeclared element emits everything: nothing is omitted where nothing is a property"
   :view :undeclared             :html "<ce-plain accent-color=\"x\" data-y=\"y\"></ce-plain>"}
  {:note "the order-independent declaration omits its property on the server too"
   :view :below-literal          :html "<ce-below data-x=\"d\"></ce-below>"}]

 ;; The BROWSER's half. The mounted element is the only place the difference
 ;; between "set as a property" and "written as an attribute" is observable —
 ;; the failure mode this whole surface exists for is renderable and inert, and
 ;; a structural assertion cannot see inert.
 ;;
 ;; `:tag` is defined by the suite as a real custom element whose `accentColor`
 ;; property is a plain accessor with NO attribute reflection, so an attribute
 ;; write is visible as a missing property rather than as nothing at all.
 :dom
 {:tag "ce-accent"
  :declared-properties #{:accent-color}
  :props {:accent-color "blue" :data-x "d" :label "wide & <deep>"}
  :expect
  {:properties         {"accentColor" "blue"}
   :absent-attributes  ["accent-color" "accentcolor" "accentColor"]
   :attributes         {"data-x" "d" "label" "wide & <deep>"}
   :absent-properties  ["label"]}}}
